WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.500
MCP A Creator and the One Hub.

2
00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:08.216
This is our entry in the GSA MCP Server and SI Agent Government Hackathon.

3
00:00:08.396 --> 00:00:16.132
It is one solution with four layers, proven on one ship: the aircraft carrier John F. Kennedy, CVN-79.

4
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.141
Here is the problem.

5
00:00:18.321 --> 00:00:25.167
The Department of Defense plans to spend more than 2.4 trillion dollars on its costliest weapon programs,

6
00:00:25.047 --> 00:00:28.818
and it has gone nine years without a clean audit opinion.

7
00:00:28.998 --> 00:00:35.242
No office can trace what a weapon system costs, from the budget request, through the contracts, to the asset.

8
00:00:35.422 --> 00:00:39.674
CVN-79 shows it. The ship has four official costs.

9
00:00:39.854 --> 00:00:50.051
The fiscal 2019 estimate is 11,341 million dollars. The statutory cap is 11,398 million.

10
00:00:50.231 --> 00:01:01.604
The Navy's baseline is 12,936 million. And the fiscal 2027 budget puts the ship at 13,409.97 million.

11
00:01:01.784 --> 00:01:05.584
None of them agree, and 59 questions stay open underneath.

12
00:01:05.764 --> 00:01:12.406
Today analysts reconcile these by hand, for weeks, and a leader still has to pick one figure and defend it.

13
00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.294
We built one solution in four layers.

14
00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:22.265
Layer one, MCP A Creator, builds a tested, governed MCP server from a question and a table.

15
00:01:22.445 --> 00:01:28.037
Layer two is the domain servers. They give agents cited access to budget documents,

16
00:01:27.917 --> 00:01:30.675
contract records and the work registers.

17
00:01:30.855 --> 00:01:35.439
Layer three is the agent orchestration environment, the front center,

18
00:01:35.319 --> 00:01:38.998
where four specialist agents propose and people decide.

19
00:01:39.178 --> 00:01:44.221
Layer four is the financial-control layer. It analyzes in exact decimals,

20
00:01:44.101 --> 00:01:51.369
reconciles every figure to its source, records where each number came from, and decides who has authority.

21
00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:53.270
No model sits in that layer.

22
00:01:53.450 --> 00:02:00.276
That is what sets this work apart: AI-assisted development, joined to deterministic financial control.

23
00:02:01.017 --> 00:02:02.792
The entry covers three tracks.

24
00:02:02.972 --> 00:02:09.992
For dataset access, the docs, analyst and ops servers query open budget, oversight and contract data.

25
00:02:10.172 --> 00:02:15.237
For service read, the One Hub server and the Ask page retrieve the state of the work:

26
00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:17.622
the gate, the tie-outs and the gap queue.

27
00:02:17.802 --> 00:02:25.287
For service write, a person's decision is submitted under their own identity, in a sandbox workspace over public data.

28
00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:28.145
All of it runs on Databricks today.

29
00:02:28.325 --> 00:02:36.155
These are the seven apps: the docs, analyst, ops and One Hub servers, MCP A Creator, the probe, and the front center.

30
00:02:36.335 --> 00:02:40.610
Each one deploys from the public GitHub repository with one command,

31
00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:44.765
and the deploy checks that each app runs the commit that was pushed.

32
00:02:45.483 --> 00:02:48.785
Six MCP servers expose 63 tools.

33
00:02:48.965 --> 00:02:58.034
The docs server reads 34 PDFs, 7,804 pages, down to the page and the table, with no model in the loop.

34
00:02:58.214 --> 00:03:02.896
The analyst server works a record that is missing data, and names what to ask for.

35
00:03:03.076 --> 00:03:09.741
The ops server serves exhibit cells, obligations and the registers. MCP A Creator built it.

36
00:03:09.921 --> 00:03:14.449
The One Hub server holds the gate, the falsification checks and the gap queue.

37
00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:20.692
The bakehouse server, also built by the Creator, reads a sales table that has nothing to do with ships.

38
00:03:20.872 --> 00:03:23.999
Five of the six are deployed, with 56 tools.

39
00:03:24.179 --> 00:03:30.303
Every input is validated, and 421 hostile calls against the read tools wrote nothing.

40
00:03:31.017 --> 00:03:33.892
MCP A Creator is the part that scales.

41
00:03:34.072 --> 00:03:36.870
Give it a question and a Unity Catalog table.

42
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.838
It profiles the table, designs one tool per question, reviews each tool,

43
00:03:41.718 --> 00:03:46.960
and generates a full project with tests, an evaluation kit and security checks.

44
00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:53.088
It deploys that project as the requester's own app, after an approval bound to the exact files.

45
00:03:53.268 --> 00:04:00.363
Skills carry the judgment, and generators write the code, so one specification gives the same project every time.

46
00:04:00.543 --> 00:04:04.388
Fifteen of fifteen generated projects pass their own tests.

47
00:04:04.568 --> 00:04:13.757
Release 1.0.0rc3 installs with one command, and an upgrade regenerates a server without overwriting the owner's edits.

48
00:04:13.937 --> 00:04:18.558
We also measured it where it was weakest. On tables it had never seen,

49
00:04:18.438 --> 00:04:21.708
its word rules read 10 of 19 questions correctly.

50
00:04:21.888 --> 00:04:27.412
So each tool now shows how it read the question. The calling model corrects it in one line,

51
00:04:27.292 --> 00:04:30.322
and code checks the correction against the table.

52
00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.875
The fourth layer is why the numbers can be trusted.

53
00:04:34.055 --> 00:04:37.560
Code reads the records and adds them in exact decimals.

54
00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.469
Each figure is tied back to its source, and to the other ledgers.

55
00:04:41.649 --> 00:04:44.990
Every number carries its document, its page and its cell.

56
00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:51.108
A gate with no model in it decides: promote, send to review, log a gap, or hold as a lead.

57
00:04:51.288 --> 00:04:54.757
And only the office that owns a record can approve a change.

58
00:04:54.937 --> 00:05:00.794
A model may plan, explain and draft. It can never add a figure, change an amount, or approve.

59
00:05:01.517 --> 00:05:06.704
The front center opens on The stack: seven capabilities, each useful on its own.

60
00:05:06.884 --> 00:05:11.184
For each one, the page shows the problem it solves, who else can use it,

61
00:05:11.064 --> 00:05:15.654
where it stands today, how it was tested, and what each result does not show.

62
00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:20.137
Security is laid out by risk, with the control that answers each one.

63
00:05:20.883 --> 00:05:25.000
Impact answers the bigger question: one carrier read to the cent,

64
00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.444
and the same agents and servers for every weapon system.

65
00:05:28.624 --> 00:05:34.927
The flow is simple. You ask. Agents plan. MCP servers fetch. The gate checks. You decide.

66
00:05:35.107 --> 00:05:42.066
A new program gets its own governed server without a developer, because each program supplies data, not software.

67
00:05:42.583 --> 00:05:48.958
Four Ford class hulls are already loaded from the budget books. The F-35 uses the same method,

68
00:05:48.838 --> 00:05:50.821
with lots in place of hulls.

69
00:05:51.001 --> 00:05:56.453
And each of the 59 programs with a fiscal 2027 acquisition report is listed,

70
00:05:56.333 --> 00:05:59.961
with what is loaded today and what it still needs.

71
00:06:00.683 --> 00:06:04.862
Moving to the next program is a set of inputs. It is never a rebuild.

72
00:06:05.042 --> 00:06:07.843
The Creator, the document reader, the gate

73
00:06:07.723 --> 00:06:14.608
and the agents are reused as they are. A program adds its documents, its contract numbers, and a role map.

74
00:06:14.788 --> 00:06:20.793
Inside the Department, the path runs from CVN-79 to the Ford class,

75
00:06:20.673 --> 00:06:27.028
then 29 Navy ship lines, the F-35, and all 59 programs on one template.

76
00:06:27.208 --> 00:06:32.800
Beyond defense, every agency has the same shape of problem: budget justifications,

77
00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:37.271
contract and grant records, and audit findings it cannot yet close.

78
00:06:38.017 --> 00:06:42.535
Back in the front center, the Brief asks what we can say about the ship's cost today.

79
00:06:42.715 --> 00:06:45.630
It works in four steps: start from your question,

80
00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:49.565
check what you can say, act on what is still open, and use the tools.

81
00:06:50.283 --> 00:06:56.576
The ship page holds the record: identity, key dates, and every milestone, with construction photographs.

82
00:06:57.317 --> 00:07:02.775
Position answers the first question a leader asks: which figure do I quote, and for what?

83
00:07:02.955 --> 00:07:07.193
The page shows where each of the four costs sits, compares every pair,

84
00:07:07.073 --> 00:07:10.839
and walks the bridge from the cap to the budget, step by step.

85
00:07:11.019 --> 00:07:14.240
It also names the difference that cannot yet be explained.

86
00:07:14.983 --> 00:07:18.684
Here is the agentic workflow, live, on the deployed app.

87
00:07:18.864 --> 00:07:25.053
From Position, we ask a specialist to explain an account period that does not tie to the transactions.

88
00:07:25.533 --> 00:07:28.396
The orchestrator routes it to the Account Analyst.

89
00:07:28.576 --> 00:07:31.851
The steps stream in, each one labeled code or model.

90
00:07:32.333 --> 00:07:38.322
The account file exceeds the closest transaction window by 770 million dollars.

91
00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:46.428
The run proposes two things: a lead for the Office of Financial Operations, and a request for the obligation detail.

92
00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:51.454
Now the access control. The signed-in user holds the program office role.

93
00:07:51.634 --> 00:07:58.788
The first proposal belongs to Financial Operations, so the app says so, and refuses to let this user apply it.

94
00:07:59.267 --> 00:08:03.655
The second proposal is owned by the program office. The user applies it,

95
00:08:03.535 --> 00:08:07.212
and the action is recorded with the role and the run number.

96
00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:10.504
Every run follows the same six stages.

97
00:08:10.684 --> 00:08:16.342
A page triggers one task on one subject. The router refuses any pair it does not know.

98
00:08:16.522 --> 00:08:20.201
Code packs the records, with every figure in exact decimals.

99
00:08:20.381 --> 00:08:26.059
One model explains. It cannot add a figure, change an amount, or change who owns an item.

100
00:08:26.239 --> 00:08:31.244
Code then grounds every number, and a model from a second family reads the answer.

101
00:08:31.424 --> 00:08:34.551
Last, only the role that owns an item can apply it.

102
00:08:34.731 --> 00:08:40.595
Unity Catalog makes that call under the user's own token. No role travels in the page.

103
00:08:40.775 --> 00:08:44.490
Four specialists run this way: the Obligation Reconciler,

104
00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:48.589
the Account Analyst, the Gap Investigator and the Brief Composer.

105
00:08:49.317 --> 00:08:55.939
Cost composition breaks the 13,409.97 million dollar total down by cost category

106
00:08:55.819 --> 00:09:01.222
and by funding stream, and shows what each contract accounts for.

107
00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:07.636
Cost growth shows the estimate in each budget submission, and the history of the statutory cap.

108
00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:14.607
Budget execution shows the obligation curve, the handoff between contracts, and the largest actions.

109
00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:20.258
The gap register asks which open questions block the number, and who can close them.

110
00:09:20.438 --> 00:09:26.057
It splits the work into what can be done now, with no access, and what needs an access request.

111
00:09:26.237 --> 00:09:29.722
The discovery queue plots each gap by what is at stake,

112
00:09:29.602 --> 00:09:35.166
and how easily it can be closed. The last table names the office that can close each one.

113
00:09:35.883 --> 00:09:41.989
From any gap, an agent drafts the request. Here the Gap Investigator works gap G52.

114
00:09:42.467 --> 00:09:45.834
It proposes collection requests addressed to the offices

115
00:09:45.714 --> 00:09:49.603
that hold the records, and the checker re-checks every condition.

116
00:09:50.067 --> 00:09:53.545
Nothing is saved until a person with the right role applies it.

117
00:09:54.283 --> 00:09:59.058
The variance register records every place where sources disagree, and the reason.

118
00:09:59.783 --> 00:10:05.753
Asset valuation explains why the value on the Navy's books will never equal program cost.

119
00:10:06.483 --> 00:10:10.398
And who builds what shows the prime contractors and the sub-award map.

120
00:10:11.117 --> 00:10:15.568
Ask the evidence takes a plain question, and answers it from the same records.

121
00:10:15.749 --> 00:10:21.342
What was obligated on the contract in fiscal 2022, and what was the largest action?

122
00:10:21.833 --> 00:10:27.827
The wait is cut. The answer comes back as a lead, with its citation and the records behind it.

123
00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:31.454
On the deployed app, Ask is an MCP host.

124
00:10:31.634 --> 00:10:37.566
It lists the tools of the docs, ops and One Hub servers, and each step names the server it used.

125
00:10:37.746 --> 00:10:42.545
Asked whether the ship can be briefed at 13,529 million dollars,

126
00:10:42.425 --> 00:10:47.370
it finds the budget line, rebuilds the exhibit, and runs the gate.

127
00:10:47.551 --> 00:10:52.618
The gate calls the result a lead to verify. It does not call it a reportable figure.

128
00:10:53.350 --> 00:10:58.923
A second question: which account periods fail to tie to the transactions, and by how much?

129
00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:03.532
The answer lists each period and its difference, with the SQL that ran.

130
00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:08.029
The work queue shows what needs a decision, what is blocked, and why.

131
00:11:08.209 --> 00:11:12.848
Code sorts every item into one lane: decide, unblock, or system.

132
00:11:13.333 --> 00:11:18.941
Each run keeps its full record: the steps, the checks code ran again, and the checker's verdict.

133
00:11:19.683 --> 00:11:24.490
Brief readiness shows what is waiting on a decision, and what the brief can stand on.

134
00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:30.260
Live, 13 of 32 CVN-79 figures tie to source records.

135
00:11:30.733 --> 00:11:39.747
The Obligation Reconciler adds 927 contract actions in exact decimals, and code re-checks eight of eight conditions.

136
00:11:40.233 --> 00:11:44.926
Its finding goes to the program office as a proposal, for a person to decide.

137
00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:50.182
The detail table filters and exports the records behind every figure.

138
00:11:50.917 --> 00:11:55.018
Sources lists the documents and lets you search them: budget books,

139
00:11:54.898 --> 00:11:58.404
oversight reports, and structured records kept as tables.

140
00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:02.669
Method explains how this is built, and why it can be challenged.

141
00:12:02.849 --> 00:12:09.655
It keeps three trust zones with a one-way door: leads, raw authoritative records, and validated figures.

142
00:12:09.835 --> 00:12:13.056
And it follows one rule. No model promotes a figure.

143
00:12:13.783 --> 00:12:18.377
Rules lists every rule code, the rule register, and the routes through the gate.

144
00:12:19.117 --> 00:12:25.089
The coverage map shows six layers of cost, where the other costs hide, and the access that would close each.

145
00:12:25.817 --> 00:12:32.798
Finally, the people who know. Answer a question opens the 59 open questions to experts at headquarters

146
00:12:32.678 --> 00:12:33.941
and in the field.

147
00:12:34.121 --> 00:12:38.158
Each question names who is likely to know: the program office,

148
00:12:38.038 --> 00:12:42.959
financial operations, the supervisor of shipbuilding, or the finance center.

149
00:12:43.139 --> 00:12:46.819
An expert writes what they know, how they know it, and how sure they are.

150
00:12:46.999 --> 00:12:51.861
The answer is recorded with their name as a lead, routed to the gap it answers,

151
00:12:51.741 --> 00:12:55.462
and never treated as a certified figure until it is checked.

152
00:12:55.642 --> 00:13:02.819
A field engineer, or a headquarters analyst who knows which ledger holds an account, can close a gap in two sentences.

153
00:13:02.999 --> 00:13:06.337
The gap register shows which office owns each question,

154
00:13:06.217 --> 00:13:10.433
the work queue routes the answer, and the run log keeps who said what.

155
00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:12.463
How do we know it works?

156
00:13:12.643 --> 00:13:17.716
On 69 sealed questions, hashed before any model saw them,

157
00:13:17.596 --> 00:13:25.190
Claude Sonnet 4.5 answers 88.4 percent with the servers, against 14.5 percent without.

158
00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:30.786
GPT-5 mini goes from 1.4 percent to 78.7.

159
00:13:30.966 --> 00:13:36.339
On questions written by an evaluator outside the team, Claude scores 25 of 30.

160
00:13:36.519 --> 00:13:43.507
A separate outside reviewer, working blind, would rely on 9 of 10 answers with the servers, and none without.

161
00:13:43.687 --> 00:13:50.567
1,770 tests pass from a clean clone, and 828 of them are hostile inputs.

162
00:13:50.747 --> 00:13:58.862
On the deployed store, 7,743 of 7,746 budget tie-outs pass.

163
00:13:59.042 --> 00:14:05.009
And the probe passes 99.9 percent of 6,193 live checks.

164
00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:07.015
It is built to run.

165
00:14:07.195 --> 00:14:12.346
Every app requires workspace sign-in. Decisions run under the user's own token.

166
00:14:12.526 --> 00:14:18.609
Tool inputs are validated and capped, identifiers are allow-listed, and parameters are bound.

167
00:14:18.789 --> 00:14:22.960
Security scans run in CI and in every generated project.

168
00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:29.112
And the probe calls every deployed server every 15 minutes, and alerts when a known answer changes.

169
00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:34.844
AI builds and explains. Code proves the number. The owning role decides.

170
00:14:35.024 --> 00:14:38.947
One ship, reconciled to the cent, and ready for the next program.

171
00:14:39.127 --> 00:14:43.293
The code, the evaluation and every page are in the repository.

